COMMAND NAME: gpssh-exkeys

Exchanges SSH public keys between hosts.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpssh-exkeys { -f <host_file>
             | -h <hostname> [-h <hostname> ...]
             | -e <host_file> -x <host_file> }

gpssh-exkeys -? | --version


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpssh-exkeys utility exchanges SSH authentication files between the
current host and the specified hosts.

This exchange is accomplished by preparing several SSH authentication
files (authorized_keys, known_hosts, and id_rsa/id_rsa.pub) for the
current user on the current host and replicating those files to the
current user on each specified host.  Assuming every host in a Greenplum
Database system is specified, the result of the authentication file
exchange is to enable the current user SSH login between any two hosts
in the Greenplum Database system (including the host on which the
gpssh-exkeys command is run) without a password.

The gpssh-exkeys utility may be used in two ways: (a) to initially
prepare a Greenplum Database system for password-less SSH access and
(b) to add hosts to an existing, previously prepared Greenplum Database
system.

To perform the initial key exchange, specify the hosts to be set up for
password-less SSH access using either the -f option (recommended) one or
more -h options -- at least one of these options must be provided.  The
SSH authentication files for the current user are replicated to the
current user on each host.  The authentication files for the current
user on each host are REPLACED with those from the current host.

To add hosts to an existing Greenplum Database system, use the -e option
to specify the existing hosts in the system and the -x option to specify
the hosts to be added to the system -- both options must be provided.
The current user's SSH authentication files are updated as required to
ensure the current user on each existing host (identified with the -e
option) continues to have SSH login access to every identified host
without a password.  The current user's authentication files are
replicated to each new host as with the initial setup above.

The gpssh-exkeys utility performs key exchange using the following 
steps:

    1) Create an RSA identification key pair for the current user if
       one does not already exist.  The public key of this pair is
       added to the current user's authorized_keys file.

    2) The host key of each host specified using the -h, -f, -e, and
       -x options is obtained using ssh-keyscan.  These keys are added
       to the current user's known_hosts file.

    3) Each host is accessed using SSH to (a) setup password-less SSH
       access for the current user from the current host and (b) obtain
       the authorized_keys, known_hosts, and id_rsa.pub files from each
       host specified using the -e option.  This access may require
       entering a login password for each host.

    4) Add keys from the id_rsa.pub files obtained from each host
       specified using the -e option to the current user's
       authorized_keys file.

    5) For each host specified using the -h, -f, and -x options, push
       the current user's authorized_keys, known_hosts, and
       id_rsa/id_rsa.pub files to the host replacing existing versions
       of those files, if any.  For each host specified using the -e
       option, merge the current user's authorized_keys and known_hosts

Greenplum recommends performing the key exchange process twice: 
once as root and once as the gpadmin user (the user designated 
to own your Greenplum Database installation). The Greenplum Database 
management scripts require that the same non-root user be created on 
all hosts in the Greenplum Database system, and the scripts must 
be able to connect as that user to all hosts without a password prompt.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-f <host_file>

Specifies the name of a file containing a list of hosts participating in
initial SSH key exchange.  Each line of the file must contain a single
host name -- the username@hostname:ssh_port format is not accepted.
Blank lines and lines beginning with '#' are considered comments and
ignored.  References to the local host are ignored with a warning.


-h <hostname>

Specifies a single host name that will participate in the SSH key
exchange.  You can use the -h option multiple times to specify multiple
host names.  References to the local host are ignored with a warning.


-e <host_file>

Specifies the name of a file containing a list of hosts participating in
an expansion SSH key exchange.  Each host is presumed an existing host
(one previously prepared using gpssh-exkeys).  Each line of the file
must contain a single host name -- the username@hostname:ssh_port format
is not accepted.  Blank lines and lines beginning with '#' are
considered comments and ignored.  Hosts specified in this file may not
also be specified in the -x host_file.  References to the local host are
ignored with a warning.


-x <host_file>

Specifies the name of a file containing a list of hosts participating in
an expansion SSH key exchange.  Each host is considered a new host (one
not previously prepared using gpssh-exkeys).  Each line of the file must
contain a single host name -- the username@hostname:ssh_port format is
not accepted.  Blank lines and lines beginning with '#' are considered
comments and ignored.  Hosts specified in this file may not also be
specified in the -e host_file.  References to the local host are ignored
with a warning.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.

--version

Displays the version of this utility.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Exchange SSH keys between all hosts listed in the file host_file:

gpssh-exkeys -f host_file


Exchange SSH keys between the hosts thumper0, thumper1, and 
thumper2:

gpssh-exkeys -h thumper0 -h thumper1 -h thumper2


Exchange SSH keys with existing hosts thumper0, thumper1, and
thumper2 adding hosts thumper3 and thumper4:

cat - >existing.hosts
thumper0
thumper1
thumper2
^D
cat - >new.hosts
thumper3
thumper4
^D
gpssh-exkeys -e existing.hosts -x new.hosts


